#!/bin/sh
# `claude`, with every tool taken away.
#
# `ClaudeCodeLLM` shells out to `claude --print ... --output-format json`, and
# `claude --print` has its full tool set by default: Read, Write, Bash, and the
# caller's working directory. Verified on 2026-09-03 -- a one-line prompt asking
# it to `echo` into a file wrote the file, with `permission_denials: []`.
#
# That matters twice.
#
# For the **measurement**, it means a pass is not one generation: the terminal
# smoke pass in the first live run took 602s across 18 turns and 47k output
# tokens, and said in its notes that it had stood a scratch tree up and run the
# tests it was writing. That is a real cost and arguably the honest one, since
# it is what `proto --backend claude-code` does today -- but it moves the work
# out of the repair loop and into the pass, which is exactly where the plan's
# headline metric cannot see it.
#
# For the **product**, it means `files.py`'s guarantee -- "the model can only
# write five paths", "model output is data, not authority" -- holds for the
# `api` backend and does not hold for this one. The guard checks what the
# returned JSON may write. It does not constrain the subprocess that returned
# it. That is a bug in proto.pilot, not in this spike, and it is not this
# spike's to fix.
#
# Use this to run the measurement as a pure generation instead:
#
#     PROTOPILOT_CLAUDE_BIN=scripts/spikes/terminal_target/claude-no-tools \
#       .venv/bin/python scripts/spikes/terminal_target/run.py --target tui --spec field_notes
#
# Run the six one way or the other, never a mix: the two produce different
# numbers and averaging them measures nothing.
#
# `--tools ""` rather than `--disallowed-tools`: a deny list has to name
# every tool, and the first attempt here missed two. The model said so --
# "only Monitor (which I won't repurpose as a shell for this) can execute
# commands" -- which is a deny list working by the model's goodwill rather
# than by the flag. An allow list of nothing has no such hole.
#
# ...except one layer out, which is where the first version of this file
# stopped and was wrong. `--tools` governs the **built-in** tools. MCP servers
# are a separate registry and survive it: with `--tools ""` alone the model
# reported it still had Google Drive, Slack and Cloudflare, and said so
# unprompted while explaining why it could not write the file. Whatever is in
# the operator's MCP config is reachable from inside a generation pass.
#
# So the isolation needs all three flags, and the check that it worked is to
# ask the model what it has rather than to trust the flags:
#
#     --tools ""                      no built-in tools
#     --strict-mcp-config             ignore every ambient MCP configuration
#     --mcp-config '{"mcpServers":{}}'  ...and supply an empty one
#
# Verified 2026-09-03: that combination reports exactly one tool,
# `StructuredOutput`, which is the one the schema needs. `--tools ""` alone
# reports the three MCP servers above.
#
# This is the same lesson as the deny-list paragraph, one level up, and it is
# worth stating plainly because the first version of this file *argued* the
# lesson and then missed the instance: an allow list is only exhaustive over
# the registry it governs, and a second registry is invisible to it.
exec claude "$@" --tools "" --strict-mcp-config --mcp-config '{"mcpServers":{}}'
